1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,979
Compliance work often lives across spreadsheets, inboxes, and disconnected folders.

2
00:00:05,979 --> 00:00:13,921
That makes it difficult to see who owns the work, what evidence exists, and which gaps need attention.

3
00:00:13,921 --> 00:00:19,000
FeDril brings those readiness activities into one organized, No-CUI workspace.

4
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:26,764
Northstar Precision Systems is a fictional company used only for this demonstration.

5
00:00:26,764 --> 00:00:33,228
Its dashboard brings together the current readiness signal, overdue work, high-priority obligations,

6
00:00:33,228 --> 00:00:35,969
and evidence status.

7
00:00:35,969 --> 00:00:44,500
Teams can start with the items that need attention instead of reconciling several trackers before every review.

8
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:51,956
The obligation queue highlights a high-priority item tied to FAR 52.204-21.

9
00:00:51,956 --> 00:00:59,382
Opening the detail view keeps the plain-language action, source reference, expected evidence, current status,

10
00:00:59,382 --> 00:01:02,575
and related work together.

11
00:01:02,575 --> 00:01:10,000
FeDril supports the review process; it does not replace qualified compliance or legal judgment.

12
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:17,373
This readiness item is assigned to Northstar's compliance manager, Priya Shah.

13
00:01:17,373 --> 00:01:23,861
A linked remediation task identifies the expected action and the due date.

14
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:33,259
The overdue state is visible in the work queue, so the team can discuss the gap, confirm the owner,

15
00:01:33,259 --> 00:01:38,500
and move the work forward through the implemented workflow.

16
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:45,161
Northstar's fictional quarterly access-review summary is represented as non-sensitive evidence metadata.

17
00:01:45,161 --> 00:01:51,575
It is associated with the selected obligation and can be reviewed alongside the requirement.

18
00:01:51,575 --> 00:01:56,936
No customer document or file content is used in this demonstration,

19
00:01:56,936 --> 00:02:03,000
and the association does not imply that an assessor has accepted the evidence.

20
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,965
FeDril's current product posture is compliance management only.

21
00:02:08,965 --> 00:02:16,795
The evidence workspace presents a No-CUI notice and identifies prohibited sensitive content before the upload area.

22
00:02:16,795 --> 00:02:21,065
This demonstration does not upload a file.

23
00:02:21,065 --> 00:02:28,500
Teams should use synthetic, redacted, or non-sensitive information and follow their own approved handling procedures.

24
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:35,300
Server-authoritative permissions determine which actions each tenant member can perform.

25
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:39,600
Compliance-relevant changes also create tenant-scoped audit events.

26
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:47,100
The audit view gives reviewers a chronological record of activity without exposing another tenant's information.

27
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:53,000
These controls support accountability; they do not certify an organization's compliance.

28
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:59,106
A generated compliance-status snapshot brings the current obligation, task, evidence,

29
00:02:59,106 --> 00:03:02,920
and risk signals into a reviewable summary.

30
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:10,040
Leadership can use that snapshot to focus the next readiness conversation and follow up on outstanding work.

31
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:16,500
The report is workflow guidance, not legal advice, certification, or a guarantee of assessment results.

32
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:22,813
FeDril helps teams organize CMMC readiness work, connect ownership and evidence metadata,

33
00:03:22,813 --> 00:03:25,451
and keep remediation visible.

34
00:03:25,451 --> 00:03:30,364
If your team is replacing fragmented trackers with a repeatable compliance-management process,

35
00:03:30,364 --> 00:03:33,000
schedule a FeDril demonstration.
